From 3ddc7c9964b69a4fa76b48902ae49e22f91d6558 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jiaxing Hu <gahing@gahingwoo.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 14:15:53 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] rocket RK3576: ROCKET_FS_KEEP, to measure the load bearing
 slice

Round 30 reported per output channel instead of per model. Zeroing the 256
bytes at groups*64 destroys all 128 channels, not the 64 that a 4-byte per
channel layout would touch, and writing a correct per-channel fp16 weight scale
fails identically. A plausible large float fails like a zero does, so it is not
a magnitude problem. That is three rounds of substituting a meaning into this
region, all failing the same way.

The size, on the other hand, has never been measured. 0x0b90 is where the
vendor's constant tail begins, which says nothing about this driver; all that is
known is that keeping 0x400..0x0b90 works and zeroing it all fails.
ROCKET_FS_KEEP=<n> keeps the first n bytes of the slice and zeroes the rest, so
a sweep finds the boundary.
---
 src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c | 28 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 28 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c b/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c
index 39db381..667f67c 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/rocket/rkt_coefs.c
@@ -821,6 +821,34 @@ rkt_fill_biases(struct rkt_ml_subgraph *subgraph,
       if (getenv("ROCKET_FS_MIDZERO") && bufsize > 0x0b90)
          memset(b8 + 0x400, 0, 0x0b90 - 0x400);
 
+      /*
+       * ROCKET_FS_KEEP=<n>: keep the first n bytes of the float surface and
+       * zero the rest of it, up to 0x0b90.
+       *
+       * The extent of what this driver actually needs there has never been
+       * measured. 0x0b90 was picked because that is where the VENDOR's constant
+       * tail begins, which says nothing about mesa. What is known is only that
+       * keeping 0x400..0x0b90 works and zeroing all of it fails, so the answer
+       * is somewhere in those 1936 bytes.
+       *
+       * Round 30 ruled out reading it as a per-channel table: changing the first
+       * 256 bytes destroys all 128 output channels uniformly rather than the 64
+       * a 4-byte-per-channel layout would touch, and substituting a plausible
+       * large float fails exactly like substituting zero. So stop assigning it a
+       * meaning and measure its size instead.
+       *
+       * Sizes worth landing on, for conv2d-cal at ic=16 oc=128 k=5:
+       *    512 = 128 floats = one per output channel
+       *   1600 = 400 floats = ic*k*k, one output channel's worth of weights
+       *   1936 = the whole slice, which must pass, and 0 must fail
+       */
+      const char *keep = getenv("ROCKET_FS_KEEP");
+      if (keep && bufsize > 0x0b90) {
+         unsigned n = strtoul(keep, NULL, 0);
+         if (n < 0x0b90 - 0x400)
+            memset(b8 + 0x400 + n, 0, (0x0b90 - 0x400) - n);
+      }
+
       /*
        * ROCKET_FS_TABLE_OVER / ROCKET_FS_HOLE: separate the two things round 28
        * changed together.
-- 
